        {
          "id": 3567602894,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "In HTTP2, we have a DATA chunk with a defined length, so, we should indeed only see multiparty-body in that field.\n\nI came to the conclusion that what this vendor was doing was valid by working out an example based on the definition that you've included.\n\nLet's start with this: `Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=magicword`.  Then I'll just jump to the end after our last body-part, which consists of headers, a CRLF, and then data.  We get into the close-delimiter, which is the delimiter followed by \"--\".  delimiter is a linebreak and then our dash boundary.  So, in all, we would append `\\r\\n--magicword--` to our data.  close-delimiter does not have a CRLF at the end of it.  The next item, transport-padding, would not allow for additional linebreaks as the last bytes.  If we chose not to add the optional epilogue, then there would be nothing else added to this data and we would not end with a linebreak.  If an implementation did include a CRLF as the last two bytes of the HTTP2 data chunk, then it would seem that is an empty epilogue.  This empty epilogue would include the CRLF as defined in multipart-body.  The epilogue would be discard-text, which is defined as 0 or more of text/CRLF.  I would conclude that both including and omitting the CRLF as the last two bytes of the HTTP2 data chunk would be valid.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5925829,
            "username": "jmasterfunk",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Jeff Dyer",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/d5d273b85ef8a2caaa41072f45b753b33b2345d0baea28001e956d43a250dbf4?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/jmasterfunk"
          },
